{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nOn 2026-09-02 the `rebar-gerrit` host (`i-00880b2c7f13527c5`, us-east-1) filled its 30-GiB gp3 root volume and took **Gerrit fully offline for ~5 hours** (~02:05–07:10 PDT). This is the FOURTH root-disk-pressure incident in a month and the FIRST to cause a complete outage: previous incidents degraded the review bot, this one killed the service, the SSM agent, and the metrics publisher simultaneously.\n\nTwo distinct defects. The second is new and is the reason nobody was paged.\n\n## Defect 1 — root disk fills faster than reclaim frees it\n\nRoot reached **29 GiB used of 30 GiB**. Every prior mitigation has reduced the rate without bounding the ceiling, so the disk still reaches 100%.\n\n## Defect 2 (NEW, and the reason this became a 5-hour outage) — the disk alarm RESET ITSELF TO OK as the host died\n\n`rebar-root-disk-pressure` is configured:\n\n* metric `rebar/host` `root_disk_used_percent`, threshold **> 85%**\n* **EvaluationPeriods = 3**\n* **TreatMissingData = `notBreaching`**\n\nThe metric is published BY AN AGENT ON THE HOST THAT IS RUNNING OUT OF DISK. Observed series (30-min max):\n\n```\n2026-09-01T23:03  92\n2026-09-01T23:33  94      <- breaching (>85)\n2026-09-02T00:03  92\n2026-09-02T00:33  91\n2026-09-02T01:03  92\n2026-09-02T01:33  90      <- LAST DATAPOINT EVER\n(no data thereafter)\n```\n\n`GerritReachable` stops at the same timestamp. The publisher died with the host.\n\nBecause the disk was breaching but had not yet accumulated **3** breaching periods, and because missing data is treated as **notBreaching**, the alarm did not fire — it transitioned **to `OK` at 01:45:10**, i.e. it went GREEN at the exact moment the host became unreachable, and is STILL green now. The only thing that fired was `rebar-gerrit-gate-down` (a black-box reachability probe) at 02:05.\n\nThis is the classic dead-man's-switch inversion: for a metric published by the failing host, \"no data\" is the strongest possible evidence of the condition, and this configuration reads it as the absence of the condition. `rebar-gerrit-data-disk-high` has the SAME `notBreaching` setting and the same inversion.\n\n## Why the prior fixes did not hold\n\nChronology (all on this host, all root disk unless noted):\n\n* **2026-08-04/06** — `sour-plugugly-rhino` (28f9-535c-ce9c-4583): root 48% -> 95%, pinned 92–96% for ~39h. Fix: pressure-triggered `docker prune` on the autodeploy no-op tick.\n* **2026-08-17** — `masonic-abeyant-stagbeetle` (3a52-2d79-320d-4fed): root 71% -> 90%, review-bot unhealthy. Title records this as happening **\"after automated-reclaim mitigation\"** — the Aug-4 prune did not prevent it.\n* **2026-08-26** — `/var/gerrit` DATA volume fill; remediation task `scowlful-bovine-dragonfly` (3e92-b7f4-dede-4c17) is **still OPEN**.\n* **2026-08-29/30** — `beton-naval-eyas` (2f46-6871-796f-41c9): correlated autodeploy alarms on this host.\n* **2026-09-01 (yesterday)** — `preodorous-temporary-piglet` (3cef-c948-68ba-40fd): root 91–95%, review clones hit ENOSPC, change 2481 burned its retry budget into a **spurious `LLM-Review -1`**.\n* **2026-09-02 (today)** — full outage, ~5h.\n\nReclaim has been made *faster* and *more triggered* each round, but nothing has ever bounded the CEILING, and no alarm survives the host it monitors.\n\n## Recovery performed (an UNWEDGE, not a fix)\n\n1. Snapshot `snap-06f35ff3a20fe4a73` of root `vol-0270fcf13709cf472`.\n2. `modify-volume` root **30 -> 60 GiB**.\n3. Reboot; AL2023 cloud-init `growpart` expanded partition + filesystem at boot.\n4. Verified: `/dev/nvme0n1p1 60G 29G 32G 48% /`; Gerrit HTTP 200 and SSM `Online` at 07:10 PDT.\n\n**The 29 GiB of content is UNCHANGED — this only bought headroom.** At the observed fill rate the larger disk postpones recurrence, it does not prevent it. Growing the volume must not be recorded as the resolution.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 [non-codebase]: `rebar-root-disk-pressure` and `rebar-gerrit-data-disk-high` no longer treat missing data as healthy — `TreatMissingData` becomes `breaching` (or `missing`), so a host that stops publishing raises rather than clears. Verified by `aws cloudwatch describe-alarms` showing the new setting, and by an induced publish-stop raising the alarm.\n- [x] AC2 [non-codebase] [REWRITTEN 2026-09-02 — the original premise was refuted by measurement; see the correction comment on this ticket]: the disk alarm RE-ENTERS alarm when the publisher dies, instead of self-clearing to OK and staying there. Original AC2 asked for \"an alarm that would have fired on the 2026-09-01T23:33 94% datapoint\"; that is unsatisfiable, because the raw 300s series has no 23:33 datapoint (the 94 reading is at 23:40) and the alarm had ALREADY been in continuous ALARM since 22:32 PDT under BOTH the old and new configuration, so nothing \"fires on\" it. What the fix actually buys, and what this AC now requires, is that after the last real datapoint at 01:40 the alarm returns to ALARM on missing data rather than remaining OK through the outage. Verified by replaying the recorded series against the live configuration (Maximum / 300s / evaluation_periods 3 / datapoints_to_alarm 2 / threshold 85 / GreaterThanThreshold / treat_missing_data breaching): window [01:40 real 85 = not breaching, 01:45 missing -> breaching, 01:50 missing -> breaching] gives 2 of 3, so ALARM at ~01:55:10 PDT — versus never under the old configuration. The replay is validated against real alarm history, predicting the 01:45 OK (actual 01:45:10) and the 22:30 ALARM (actual 22:32).\n- [x] AC3: the top consumer of the 29 GiB root working set is identified and named with measured byte totals (the `du`/`docker system df` breakdown could not be captured during recovery because SSM invocations hung on the loaded box).\n- [ ] AC4: root usage has a bounded CEILING, not merely a faster reclaim — a mechanism that cannot exceed a configured high-water mark regardless of build burst rate. Verified by a test or simulation driving the documented fill rate and showing usage plateaus below the bound.\n- [x] AC5: the still-open `/var/gerrit` remediation `scowlful-bovine-dragonfly` is either completed or explicitly superseded by this ticket's fix, so the data volume is not left with the same unbounded shape.\n- [x] AC6 [non-codebase]: an operator runbook entry covers \"host is wedged and SSM is ConnectionLost\", recording the shell-free recovery path used here (snapshot -> modify-volume -> reboot -> cloud-init growpart) and stating explicitly that it is an unwedge requiring a follow-up reclaim.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The EC2 status checks stayed `ok` throughout: the hypervisor and instance networking were healthy, so instance-check alarms cannot detect this class.\n- The instance is `unlimited` burstable with ~440 CPU credits and 0 surplus, so CPU throttling was ruled out as a cause.\n- TCP 443 stayed OPEN while HTTP returned 504 then timed out — the reverse proxy survived while its upstream died, which is why black-box probes saw a slow failure rather than a refusal.\n\n## Completion evidence (2026-09-03)\n\n- **AC2** — MET. Replayed the recorded series against the live configuration (Maximum / 300s / evaluation_periods 3 / datapoints_to_alarm 2 / threshold 85 / GreaterThanThreshold / treat_missing_data breaching). Window [01:40 real 85 = not breaching, 01:45 missing -> breaching, 01:50 missing -> breaching] gives 2 of 3, so ALARM at ~01:55:10 PDT versus never under the old configuration. The replay model is validated against real alarm history: it predicts the 01:45 OK (actual 01:45:10) and the 22:30 ALARM (actual 22:32).\n- **AC3** — MET. Root 28G: /var 26G, of which /var/lib/docker 17G (overlay2 16G across 67 layer directories), /var/tmp 3.6G, /var/log 1.8G (journal 1.7G). Docker's own accounting claimed ~9.5 GB with ZERO dangling images, so ~6.5 GB was orphaned overlay2 layer state prune cannot reach — which is why four rounds of prune-based mitigation never held.\n- **AC5** — MET, by doing the work inside this ticket per the operator's decision. Gerrit change 2586 (MERGED) adds a `/var/gerrit` non-`site` debris census publishing `rebar/host:data_disk_debris_bytes`, and alarm `rebar-gerrit-data-disk-debris` (1 GiB, 2-of-3, breaching), **now APPLIED and live in production reading 0 bytes**. Plus runbook `infra/runbooks/gerrit-data-volume-reclaim.md` and a pointer from review-bot-ops.md. The enforceable half is detection; the evidence-location policy is advisory and labelled as such in the tree, because rebar cannot stop a shell on the box writing where it likes.\n- **AC6** — MET. Gerrit change 2549 (MERGED) adds `infra/runbooks/gerrit-host-wedged-ssm-lost.md`, leading with the UNWEDGE-not-a-fix framing and the 29-GiB-before/29-GiB-after argument, plus a cross-reference from review-bot-ops.md. The root volume id was verified against the live API before landing, since the data volume id is hardcoded in sibling runbooks and the two are dangerous to confuse.\n\n## The two ACs that remain, and why\n\n- **AC1** — HALF met. `describe-alarms` confirms both disk alarms are live at `treat_missing_data = breaching` (root_disk_pressure 300/3/2, gerrit_data_disk_high 300/3/2). The second half asks for an INDUCED publish-stop raising the alarm, which is a deliberate production test: stopping the publisher stops ALL host metrics, so it would drive roughly twelve alarms into ALARM and page for a non-incident. Awaiting an operator decision on whether to run it, or to accept the AC2 replay — which proves the missing-data semantics against real recorded data — as sufficient.\n- **AC4** — NOT met, and owned elsewhere by design. The bounded ceiling is Defect 1, which the operator-approved plan decomposed into epic `6202-e1c7-c57f-4897` (endowed-upset-scaup); that epic's own description states \"Defect 2 stays on that bug and was fixed/landed/applied live. This epic is Defect 1.\" The epic is open, linked, and still has NO CHILDREN. Awaiting an operator decision on whether this ticket closes on Defect 2 with AC4 recorded as epic-owned, or stays open until 6202 delivers."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788470890165933001,"uuid":"56bbe651-0607-42ed-af2f-ae22b2e1c72b"}